# Line endings are settled HERE, not by each contributor's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# Why this file exists
# --------------------
# .editorconfig has said `end_of_line = lf` for `[*]` since the beginning, but
# .editorconfig only binds editors — git never read it. What git read instead
# was whatever `core.autocrlf` the contributor happened to have, and on Windows
# that is `true` by default. The blobs in this repository are all LF (measured:
# zero tracked text files contain a CR), so nothing was broken yet — but two
# things were true and neither was safe:
#
#   1. The WORKING TREE on Windows is CRLF, and a shell script with a CRLF
#      shebang does not run on Linux. Measured from this checkout:
#        $ wsl bash -lc './scripts/sync-site.sh'
#        /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#      WSL reads the same files as Windows does, so anyone developing that way
#      hits it on the first script they run.
#   2. Nothing stopped a CRLF blob from being COMMITTED. `core.autocrlf=false`
#      on Windows — a common setting, and the one people are told to use when
#      autocrlf mangles something — commits exactly what is on disk. The repo
#      stayed clean by luck, not by rule.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` makes both answers the repository's, not the machine's:
# every text file is stored LF and checked out LF everywhere.
#
# One limit worth knowing before you trust this to fix an existing file:
# `text=auto` converts on check-IN, and git documents that it does nothing to a
# path that was already committed with CRLF. `git add --renormalize` will report
# no change for such a file. Those have to be converted by hand, once.
#
# Verify rather than trust the attribute — an attribute that does not match is
# silent. Check the bytes git would hand out:
#   git checkout-index -f --prefix=/tmp/eolcheck/ scripts/sync-site.sh
#   xxd /tmp/eolcheck/scripts/sync-site.sh | head -2

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── The genuine exceptions ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Windows script hosts are the one place CRLF is not cosmetic: cmd.exe and
# older PowerShell hosts can mis-parse an LF-only batch file. None of these
# exist in the tree today; the rule is written down so the next one added does
# not have to rediscover it.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# ── Fixtures are data, not source ─────────────────────────────────────────
# e2e/fixtures/ is the corpus the file-type, viewer and upload suites run
# against. Their bytes are the thing under test, so git must have no opinion
# about them at all — `-text` means never convert, in either direction, however
# text-like a .csv or .xml fixture looks to the heuristic.
e2e/fixtures/**  -text -eol
# The encrypted folders `filex decrypt` is tested against: ciphertext, a
# long-name sidecar and a plaintext stray whose SHA-256 is pinned — one
# converted line ending and the round trip reports corruption.
backend/internal/e2edecrypt/testdata/folders/** -text -eol
# The same, written the other way round: folders the Go writer (`filex
# encrypt`) made, which the browser code is tested against.
backend/internal/e2edecrypt/testdata/folders-go/** -text -eol
# The vault (encryption level 3) as a storage holds it, written by
# gen_vault_vectors.mjs: packs and index files are ciphertext pinned by SHA-256
# (docs/E2E-VAULT-FORMAT.md -> "Test vectors").
backend/internal/e2edecrypt/testdata/vault/** -text -eol
# Vaults one implementation wrote for the other to open: vault-go by the Go
# writer, vault-web by the browser's (vault_crossfixture_test.go,
# web/tests/lib/e2eVaultCrossFixtures.test.ts).
backend/internal/e2edecrypt/testdata/vault-go/** -text -eol
backend/internal/e2edecrypt/testdata/vault-web/** -text -eol

# ── Binaries ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `text=auto` already guesses these correctly, but a guess is a thing that can
# be wrong once and corrupt a file silently. Say it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.tiff  binary
# The HEIC the server decodes at boot (enginebin/heic_probe.heic) is compiled
# into the binary: one converted byte and every install reads "cannot decode".
*.heic  binary
*.heif  binary
*.avif  binary
*.ico   binary
*.psd   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.mp3   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.pptx  binary
*.odt   binary
*.ods   binary
*.epub  binary
*.glb   binary
*.stl   binary
